WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.360
So how did this happen?

00:02.360 --> 00:07.040
Why am I sitting in front of a computer in a white mass

00:07.040 --> 00:10.720
trying to be detected by a cheap webcam?

00:10.720 --> 00:14.600
Well, when I'm not fighting the coded gaze as a poet of code,

00:14.600 --> 00:17.840
I'm a graduate student at the MIT Media Lab.

00:17.840 --> 00:20.480
And there I have the opportunity to work

00:20.480 --> 00:22.840
on all sorts of whimsical projects,

00:22.840 --> 00:26.080
including the Aspire Mirror, a project I did

00:26.080 --> 00:29.920
so I could project digital masks onto my reflection

00:29.920 --> 00:32.240
so in the morning, if I wanted to feel powerful,

00:32.240 --> 00:33.760
I could put on a lion.

00:33.760 --> 00:37.320
If I wanted to be uplifted, I might have a quote.

00:37.320 --> 00:40.320
So I used generic facial recognition software

00:40.320 --> 00:43.440
to build the system, but found that it was really hard

00:43.440 --> 00:47.840
to test it unless if I wore a white mask.

00:47.840 --> 00:52.200
Unfortunately, I've run into this issue before.

00:52.200 --> 00:55.160
When I was an undergraduate at Georgia Tech studying

00:55.160 --> 00:58.640
computer science, I used to work on social robots.

00:58.640 --> 01:02.360
And one of my tasks was to get a robot to play peek-a-boo,

01:02.360 --> 01:06.040
a simple turn-taking game where partners covered their face

01:06.040 --> 01:08.480
and then uncover it saying peek-a-boo.

01:08.480 --> 01:11.360
The problem is peek-a-boo doesn't really work

01:11.360 --> 01:15.400
if I can't see you, and my robot couldn't see me.

01:15.400 --> 01:19.440
But I barred my roommate's face to get the project done,

01:19.440 --> 01:21.960
submitted the assignment, and figured, you know what?

01:21.960 --> 01:25.240
Somebody else will solve this problem.

01:25.240 --> 01:28.480
Not too long after, I was in Hong Kong

01:28.480 --> 01:31.920
for an entrepreneurship competition.

01:31.920 --> 01:34.640
The organizers decided to take participants

01:34.640 --> 01:36.960
on a tour of local startups.

01:36.960 --> 01:39.720
One of the startups had a social robot,

01:39.720 --> 01:41.680
and they decided to do a demo.

01:41.680 --> 01:44.640
The demo worked on everybody until it got to me,

01:44.640 --> 01:46.680
and you can probably guess it.

01:46.680 --> 01:49.640
It couldn't detect my face.

01:49.640 --> 01:52.040
I asked the developers what was going on,

01:52.040 --> 01:55.640
and it turned out we had used the same generic facial

01:55.640 --> 01:57.720
recognition software.

01:57.720 --> 01:59.720
Halfway around the world, I learned

01:59.720 --> 02:03.280
that algorithmic bias can travel as quickly

02:03.280 --> 02:07.400
as it takes to download some files off of the internet.

